{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Every vow, and every oath of binding, to afflict the soul.</b> Scripture teaches you that the Torah's granting the husband authority to annul his wife's vows does not apply to all vows in the world, but only to matters involving affliction of soul — for example, that she not bathe, or not adorn herself in colored garments, or not eat meat, or not drink wine, or not apply kohl, or not preen herself, and similar things. And even if she forbade upon herself the fruits of a certain shopkeeper, he may annul — all the more so matters between him and her concerning modesty, as written, 'between a man and his wife.' And in these two respects the husband has the power to annul, because she is bound to him in two things: in matters of affliction of soul, that he should see her beautiful and that she not suffer affliction or distress — as our Sages, of blessed memory, said, that she was called 'the mother of all living' (אם כל חי): she was given for life, not for distress; matters between him and her — it need not be said that she is bound to him in them. And certainly any woman who vows in those two respects vows with her husband's consent, since she is bound to him in them. But other matters beyond these in which the woman has vowed — the husband has no power to annul; rather, she requires a sage's release through regret, like the rest of people."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:03.334915598Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}